  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 AM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nPowerful upper low is now lifting out across southwest KS/OK \nPanhandle with a strong upper jet extending from the TX Panhandle \nthrough Central KS. At the surface, low is located near Dodge \nCity with a weak cold front/trough extending from the low into \nnorth central KS and southeast NE. \n\nStrong 850-700mb theta-e advection is resulting in a large area of \nshowers and storms moving across central and eastern OK and will \nlift across the Flint Hills into eastern KS this morning. With \nextreme PW values still in place, very high rainfall rates will\nbe likely with any of the activity this morning, which could \nlead to some flooding. \n\nUpper low will quickly slide east and start get sheared apart today. \nAs this occurs, surface low and associated cold front/trough \nwill sink south. So the main area of rainfall for today through \nthe early evening hours will be in the wrap around region of \nthis system as the strong mid level frontogenesis slides south.\nAny of this activity will likely still produce high rainfall \nrates and due to the slow movement, flooding will remain the \nmain risk through the late afternoon/early evening hours. Also \nfeel like there will be a chance for the stronger storms to \nproduce some small hail given cooler mid/upper temps and decent \ndeep layer shear. The main limiting factor will be poor mid \nlevel lapse rates.  \n\nShowers and storms will continue to sink south tonight as the upper \ntrough also slides southeast and by Fri morning, bulk of the \nrainfall will be south of the forecast area. This will leave dry \nconditions in place through the weekend as we eventually get into \nsome northwest flow aloft. To start the work week, a deep upper \ntrough will stretch from eastern Ontario/western Quebec and down \nthrough the Great Lakes. This will leave upper ridging in place from \nthe Desert Southwest through the Southern Plains. We look to get \nback into a warming trend for Tue into Wed as the sfc high shifts \neast allowing Plains to get back into some return flow.",
